topic.p:
Assignment, arguments and returns require the same ordered variant type.
There is no implicit conversion between a variant and its options, between different variants, or from a variant to object.
Use an explicit cast or of expression:

    var number = cast variant<int, string> 42;
    var text = "hello" of variant<int, string>;
    var reordered = cast variant<string, int> number;
    var widened = text of variant<double, string, int>;

The active values are respectively 42, "hello", 42 and "hello".

value as T* or value as T^ requires that exact pointer option.
value as T? requires an option of T or T? and accepts either when active.
A different active option returns null.
Weak casts to a variant or to a nonnullable value type are unsupported.

    var plain = cast variant<int, int?, string> 7;
    var optional : int? = 8;
    var present = optional of variant<int, int?, string>;
    var empty : int? = null;
    var missing = cast variant<int, int?, string> empty;

plain as int? returns 7, present as int? returns 8, and missing as int? returns null.
Strong extraction remains exact: cast int? plain throws.

variant in VALUE extracts the active payload and has static type object.
The compiler requires VALUE to have a variant type; an object containing a native-provided variant box is not accepted.
The operand is evaluated once. Null pointer, empty optional and null object payloads produce null.

    var number = 42 of variant<int, string>;
    var payload : object = variant in number;
    var result = cast int payload;

result is 42. The ordinary cast operates on the extracted integer payload.

If object is not an option, cast object value is a compiler error, just like a cast to any other undeclared option.
Workflow has no conversion that boxes a complete variant as object.
Use variant in value to extract the active payload instead.
This function is rejected at compile time:

    func f(x:variant<int, string>):void
    {
      var y = cast object x;
    }

Casting an object-typed value back to a variant without an exact object option is rejected at compile time.
There is no dynamic recovery cast.

The six comparison operators compare active payloads, including a variant and a nonvariant operand or two different variant types.
They do not order by option position or require a common variant type.

    var a = 3 of variant<int, string>;
    var b = 3.0 of variant<double, bool>;
    var text = "three" of variant<int, string>;

a == b and a == 3.0 are true.
a != text is true, while all four relational comparisons between a and text are false.
